137:**Observed:** all eight interventions change only the targeted area's age/atLeast; the other **24/24** area records remain identical. Global age can change. Across Phase 2 and Phase 3, an area lookup keyed by **that area's effective answered IDs and yes count** has **679/679** repeat-key comparisons without conflicts, including cases in which other areas change.
153:These aggregate fits pool repeated and deliberately extreme probes; the denominator is not a population sample. At ascending count 17, the four ages are 12,12,8,0 but overall is 11. At all-one, age 3 has overall 18 and areas 10,18,10,8; age 12 has overall 33 and areas 20,16,20,36. The first exact weighted-mean equality would require all weight on Cognitive, which predicts 16 for the second. Even rounding cannot reconcile this pair. The supported explanation is a separate overall norm projection over the answered-ID set.
155:**Exact observed per-area lookup for the fixed age-12 ID set.** The complete set contains 9 Physical, 9 Cognitive, 10 Language and 11 Social-Emotional answers, including calibration in its actual domain. Each cell is months, with `+` for atLeast. A dash means the count exceeds that area's item count or was unobserved. All observed entries agree across repeated profiles.
172:**Nonmonotonic area edge behavior:** unlike the numeric portions of the cumulative sweeps, this full lookup reveals low-score drops: Cognitive zero yes gives 8 months, two yes gives 2, three yes gives 0, then four yes gives 12; Social-Emotional zero/two yes gives 6 months, three yes gives 0, then four gives 8. Final controls change only that area's cumulative yes count, holding every other answer at the same typical baseline, and reproduce these drops. See [Cognitive count 2](raw/api/scoring/D3_age12_Cognitive_count02_others_typical.json), [count 3](raw/api/scoring/D3_age12_Cognitive_count03_others_typical.json), [count 4](raw/api/scoring/D3_age12_Cognitive_count04_others_typical.json), [Social count 3](raw/api/scoring/D3_age12_Social_Emotional_count03_others_typical.json), and the zero controls in D2. This is a scoring pathology, not noise in repeated calls. A count lookup explains it exactly but a smooth monotone developmental model does not. The cumulative B tests did not expose it because their early whole reports were below_floor.
207:The empirically exact age-12 lookup for the full 39-ID set is:
237:**Floor:** chronological age changes whether an age is exposed. The same 28-yes age-12 vector yields age 14 at the tested chronological ages 0,1,2,3,6,12–16, but below-floor at 17–24,36,48. An uncapped age 17 is `building` at chronological age 20 (ratio .85), and below-floor at age 21. This supports a lower reporting boundary near **85%** of chronological age. It is an inference about the tested uncapped vectors, not a fully recovered floor algorithm: the underlying pre-rounded age is hidden in floor responses. Capped age 33 is still `building` at chronological age 48 (ratio .6875), so a universal `ratio<.85→below_floor` rule is false. [Boundary .85](raw/api/scoring/M_band_age20_count32.json), [below it](raw/api/scoring/M_band_age21_count32.json), [cap exception](raw/api/scoring/M_age48_same_age12_all_one.json).
239:**Ceilings:** `capped:true` means saturation for the supplied answered-ID set, rather than a fixed age limit. Ages 3/12/24/36 all-one initial sets cap at 18/33/44/46. At age 12, answering additional extension items can lift the cap to 42,45,53. The identical devAge 33 can be capped before extension and uncapped after eight zero extension answers. An area's `atLeast` is its own saturation flag; it can be true when the global cap is false. The precise ceiling formula remains unresolved.
243:**Observed trigger at age 12, fixed full 39-ID list:** binary search in ascending-rate, descending-rate and client order gives the same boundary. The first two orderings deliberately disagree about which milestones are yes.
