389:**PARTLY REAL — real computation with defective low-score behavior and cosmetic gating.** The strongest three pieces of evidence are: **(1)** the ascending count lookup predicts 40/40 descending and 6/6 randomized overall scores, while expected-rate totals recover chronological age in 4/4 controls and a published skill norm predicts 11/11 uncapped ages; **(2)** fixed-answer controls reproduce Cognitive ages 8→2→0→12 as successes rise 1→2→3→4, and all-zero profiles can produce on_pace/ahead ages, establishing material scoring defects rather than random response noise; **(3)** the public API supplies actual answer-sensitive detail and skills, while the saved free client locks those values and inserts literal blurred 88 tiles. Thus the underlying report is substantially computed, not merely a fixed decorative result, but it does not meet a consistent monotone developmental-model interpretation at low scores. A single ratio rule explains every numeric band; extension/foreign answers alter scores and ceilings predictably. The unexplained minimum-age/floor behavior and coarse area calibration limit interpretation, and computational evidence does not establish validated developmental measurement.

